BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR AN AUTHOR SOCIAL WITH EVA JURCZYK AND SAMANTHA M. BAIL
 EY
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261109T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261109T203000
UID:bm-6528020261109@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Eva_and_S
 am___1_.png?cb=1791402059
DESCRIPTION:[Author Social]\nJoin author Eva Jurczyk in conversation with 
 Samantha M. Bailey for an Author Social!\nThe Paris Files\nis a sophistica
 ted\, character-driven thriller in which a romantic rendezvous in Paris tu
 rns disastrous after the disappearance of a new lover.\nIn the tradition o
 f\nBig Little Lies\n\, this story unfolds entirely through police intervie
 ws\, where threads of the just-out-of-reach truth tangle and snag as we he
 ar from neighbours\, friends and witnesses. Contradictory accounts\, secre
 ts and an unreliable narrator make for a deeply twisty\, up-all-night myst
 ery.\nFree to attend\, but we have limited spots -  please\nRSVP HERE\n.\
 nEva Jurczyk\nwas born in Katowice\, Poland and wound up halfway around th
 e world in a Canadian city that often masquerades as New York in the movie
 s. As her day job\, she buys books\, building library collections for the 
 University of Toronto Libraries\, and by night she writes mystery novels i
 ncluding the bestselling 6:40 to Montreal. She lives in Toronto with her h
 usband and son.\nShortlisted for Canada Reads 2025\,\nSamantha M. Bailey\n
 is an award-nominated\, USA TODAY\, and #1 international bestselling autho
 r of psychological suspense. Her work has been sold in fourteen countries\
 , translated into more than ten languages\, and optioned for screen. She l
 ives in Toronto\, where she can usually be found tapping away at her compu
 ter or curled up on her couch with a book. Samantha is currently working o
 n her next novel.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE VEHICULE PRESS FALL LAUNCH
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261111T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261111T200000
UID:bm-6611220261111@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/vehicule.
 png?cb=1793716716
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nVéhicule Press and Flying Books invite you to 
 a triple book launch!\nJoin us Wednesday November 11 at 6:30pm to celebrat
 e the publication of\nMy Summer Vacation\nby Zeenat Nagree\,\nBlack Satin\
 nby Stanley Péan\, translated by David Homel\, and\nThe Colour of Time\n\
 , or\nthe Incredible Story of Mary Gallagher\nby Eric Dupont\, translated 
 by Peter McCambridge.\nHosted by Carmine Starnino\, the event will feature
  readings by Zeenat Nagree\, David Homel and Eric Dupont.\nThis event is f
 ree and open to all.\n***\nAbout My Summer Vacation\n:\nAn auto-fictional 
 account of a childhood ending in turn-of-the-century India\, delivered by 
 an entrancing new voice.\nWhen at year’s end a schoolteacher asks her pu
 pils to write an essay about their vacations over the summer\, twelve-year
 -old Zeenat turns the routine exercise into a daily exploration of her inn
 ermost thoughts. It is the dawn of a new millennium\, and she’s coming o
 f age in a politically unstable Mumbai that is still resolutely Bombay to 
 her Muslim community. Though the world beyond her window is full of change
 \, Zeenat spends long hours bored inside her family apartment\, preoccupie
 d with the cockroaches under the fridge\, the pigeon’s nest above the cu
 pboard\, the shared toilet in the building\, and the American shows on TV.
  At night\, she reads English books in the kitchen as she questions where 
 she fits between languages in this post-colonial society. But as summer dr
 ags on\, the boredom of her routine gives way to anxieties around the plac
 e of women in society\, the violence that lurks underneath their daily liv
 es\, and the anticipation of childhood’s end.\n***\nAbout Black Satin:\n
 T\nhe elegant portraits in this book\, as the author says\, “uncover the
  lives of women jazz artists who have been unfairly neglected\, underestim
 ated\, even cancelled by the leading historians of jazz–most of them men
 .” Pianists\, singers\, composers\, harpists\, trumpeters\, they gave th
 eir all to this music born from the experience of Black people in America.
  Stanley Péan shines a light on fifteen women musicians who may have know
 n their share of glory during their lifetime\, but who have since been rel
 egated to the footnotes of jazz history\, despite what they brought to it\
 , individually and collectively. In these essays\, Ma Rainey\, Lillian Har
 din Armstrong\, Valaida Snow\, Hazel Scott\, Mary Lou Williams\, Bessie Sm
 ith\, Dorothy Donegan\, Dorothy Ashby\, Ina Ray Hutton\, Clora Larea Bryan
 t\, Melba Liston\, Jutta Hipp\, Shirley Scott\, and Geri Allen reclaim the
 ir rightful place.\nStanley Péan concludes Black Satin with an essay on a
 bolitionist Harriet Tubman\, expanding the book’s field. He states of Tu
 bman\, “She died over a century ago\, yet she has continued to inspire a
 rtists of all backgrounds\, working in all disciplines. Though it has been
  durable\, her role as a muse is a neglected aspect of her legacy.”\n***
 \nAbout The Colour of Time\, or the Incredible Story of Mary Gallagher:\nS
 ightings of the ghost of Mary Gallagher\, who was murdered\, are reported 
 every six years in Montreal\, three days after Quebec’s Saint-Jean-Bapti
 ste holiday…\nFrom the thrills and spills of the Expo 67 World’s Fair 
 to the brothels\, taverns\, and trained bears of Mary Gallagher’s 19th c
 entury Griffintown district of Montreal\, The Colour of Time is a larger-t
 han-life saga. One of the hallmarks of Eric Dupont’s writing is the juxt
 aposition of the supernatural and real worlds—his trademark magical real
 ism. This fabulous trip through time by the author of the bestselling Song
 s for the Cold of Heart is proof positive that history is no match for des
 tiny\, and that all of the future exists in the past.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF WHOOSH
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261112T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261112T200000
UID:bm-6433220261112@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Whoosh.pn
 g?cb=1790278585
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin Flying Books and Goose Lane for the launch
  of\nWhoosh\nby Michael Trussler\, featuring special guests Cassidy McFadz
 ean and Daniel Scott Tysdal\, hosted by Jim Johnstone.\nPart exploration o
 f ecological collapse\, part memoir undressing a late-life Autism diagnosi
 s.\n\nMichael Trussler’s\nWhoosh\nis an unwavering collection of “ramb
 les / more rogue than feral.” Bypassing the limitations of linear though
 t\, this unforgettable book is a maelstrom of the contemporary moment that
 ’s simultaneously urgent and adrift\, floating and incisive.\n\nTrussler
  draws influence from modern artistic movements\, political insecurity\, t
 he internet\, and environmental grief to dovetail both the unexpected and 
 the intimately familiar. Lyric and experimental\, these blazing poems coll
 apse in on themselves and each other\, lines and thoughts expanding and co
 lliding to perfectly embody the connectedness of our world’s chaos.\nMic
 hael Trussler’s work engages with the beauty and violence of the twentie
 th and twenty-first centuries from a neurodivergent\, fluid perspective. H
 is writing encompasses several genres and modes of expression\, ranging fr
 om the lyrical to the avant-garde. Trussler teaches English at the Univers
 ity of Regina and is the author of seven books\, including 1\n0:10\; The H
 istory Forest\n\, winner of the Saskatchewan Book Award for Poetry\; the s
 hort fiction collection\nEncounters\,\nwinner of the Saskatchewan Book of 
 the Year Award\; and a memoir entitled\nThe Sunday Book\,\nwhich won the S
 askatchewan Book Award in both the Non-Fiction and City of Regina categori
 es. Deeply compelled by the natural world\, Trussler hikes in the Canadian
  Rockies at every opportunity.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR OUR NOVEMBER FICTION BOOK CLUB
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261117T203000
UID:bm-6596220261116@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/NOV_2026_
 FICTION_BOOK_CLUB.jpg?cb=1792189188
DESCRIPTION:[Book Club]\nEtna\nby Paul Yoon\, is a story told through the 
 eyes of an ex-military dog. After surviving years of a devastating war\, E
 tna decides to leave the men he has fought alongside for years and sets ou
 t on an odyssey across a war-torn country in search of home\, hope\, and a
  life beyond conflict. Along the way\, he encounters other animals and hum
 ans who are attempting to figure out how to start again. What makes a life
  when there is no home to go back to? How do we begin to trust each other 
 again after such profound loss? This is a novel about the power of an idea
 \, about never giving up\, and ultimately a novel about finding hope in th
 e most dire of times.\nJoin us on Monday\, November 16th and Tuesday\, Nov
 ember 17th for a moving conversation. We hope to see you there.\nYour book
  club ticket includes:\nA glass of wine (or another beverage of your choic
 e).\nA thoughtful conversation with our brilliant moderator\, Lee Suksi.\n
 A $20 credit toward any book in the shop.*\nGET YOUR TICKETS HERE!\nA few 
 details:\nTicket price includes HST and fees.\n*$20 book credit is valid o
 n the night of attendance only (non-refundable and non-transferable).\nWin
 e is served on the night of the event (ID required).\nTickets are non-refu
 ndable within 7 days of the event\; due to limited capacity and planning\,
  we’re not able to transfer tickets to future book club sessions.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF NOBODY'S MOTHER BY LINDSEY HARRINGTON
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261119T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261119T200000
UID:bm-6629920261119@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Nobody_s_
 Mother___Flying_Books.jpg?cb=1792688844
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin us at Flying Books on November 19th for th
 e launch of\nNobody's Mother: A Childfree Memoir\nby Lindsey Harrington\, 
 hosted by Megan Parsons.\nTo be or not to be (a mother). That is the quest
 ion. \nGrowing up in rural\, Catholic Newfoundland in the 1990s\, Lindsey 
 Harrington had no idea that children were a choice.\nNobody’s Mother\nch
 ronicles her realization and subsequent journey to decide upon and live a 
 childfree life.\n\nVulnerable\, intimate\, and insightful\, this memoir ex
 plores the societal expectations of women\, mother-daughter dynamics\, fem
 ale friendship\, reproductive autonomy\, mental health\, and the maternal 
 role of women in romantic relationships. \n\nIt was only through finding f
 encesitter and childfree community\, discussions\, and writing that Lindse
 y overcame her issues and built a life that didn't center children.\nLinds
 ey Harrington is a Nova Scotia-based\, Newfoundland-born writer of nonfict
 ion\, fiction\, and poetry. She has been longlisted for the CBC Nonfiction
  Prize and has published with CBC\,\nthe Toronto Star\n\, and\nBlank Space
 s\nMagazine. She loves exploring societal issues through a personal lens\,
  as she does in her debut\,\nNobody's Mother: A Childfree Memoir\n. She sh
 ares her Dartmouth home with her husband\, a typewriter named Audrey\, and
  two enormous dogs?but for the record she's not a dog mom! When she's not 
 writing\, you can find her crafting\, cooking\, with her nose in a book\, 
 or out in nature. Follow her @lindseyharringtonwriter.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR AN AUTHOR SOCIAL WITH MATT MCMANUS & LUKE SAVAGE
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261123T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261123T203000
UID:bm-6609720261123@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Matt_McMa
 nus_Author_Social.jpg?cb=1792446112
DESCRIPTION:[Author Social]\nJoin authors Matt McManus in conversation wit
 h Luke Savage for an Author Social!\nLiberalism and socialism have long be
 en understood as mortal enemies\, but both developed out of the Enlightenm
 ent and are committed to the importance of reason\, freedom\, and flourish
 ing for all.\nIn\nWhat is Liberal Socialism?\nMcManus makes the case for r
 etrieving a long-forgotten tradition of liberal socialist thought. The boo
 k lays out the strengths and weaknesses of liberal socialism in the contex
 t of the tumultuous 21st century\, arguing that it may be an overlooked so
 urce of hope\, one deserving of reconsideration in an era of right wing po
 pulism.\nFree to attend\, but we have limited spots -  please\nRSVP HERE\
 n.\nMatt McManus\nis an Assistant Professor at Spelman College. He is a f
 requent contributor to\nJacobin\nand\nDamage\nmagazines and the author of\
 nThe Political Theory of Liberal Socialism\namongst other works.\nLuke Sav
 age\nis a writer and essayist whose work has appeared in\nThe Atlantic\, T
 he New Statesman\, The Washington Post\n\, and\nThe Guardian\n. Born in St
  John’s\, Newfoundland\, he studied political science\, history\, and ph
 ilosophy at the University of Toronto\, where he edited Canada’s largest
  student newspaper\nThe Varsity\n. Having worked for several years as an i
 nvestigative reporter covering Canadian politics and the far right\, Luke 
 has been a staff writer at\nJacobin\nsince 2018 – where he is a frequent
  commentator on American\, British\, and Canadian politics. He is co-host 
 of the popular\nMichael and Us\npodcast\, a twice-weekly show about politi
 cs\, cinema\, and our crumbling world.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE THE LAUNCH OF SLODETHLAND ’66 BY JEAN MARC AH-SE
 N AND ANDRÉ BABYN
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261123T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261123T200000
UID:bm-6701620261123@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Book_Laun
 ch_Slodethland__1_.jpg?cb=1793716655
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin Flying Books and Enfield & Wizenty to cele
 brate the Toronto launch of\nSlodethland ’66\nby Jean Marc Ah-Sen and An
 dré Babyn.  Admission is free. Please RSVP to marketing@greatplainspres
 s.ca.\nNew York City\, 1966. Danica Klimmeck\, a painter and life model ne
 wly arrived in America\, sees a chance to escape poverty when her ex-husba
 nd\, John Slodeth\, leaves her the Aleph Null\, a burlesque club serving t
 he city’s gay community in defiance of the Liquor Authority.\n\nAs Danic
 a learns to manage unruly dancers and run a business\, she rekindles her l
 ove of painting and becomes a rising talent in the Pop Art world. But when
  she leaves New York behind for small-town life\, she discovers that escap
 e isn’t as simple as it seems. Between needy neighbours\, an alcoholic c
 owboy named Tenn\, and the strange persistence of both the Aleph Null and 
 her dead ex-husband\, Danica finds that some things refuse to stay buried.
 \nJean Marc Ah-Sen is the author of\nGrand Menteur\, In the Beggarly Style
  of Imitation\, and Kilworthy Tanner.\nHis writing has appeared in\nMcSwee
 ney’s Quarterly Concern\, Literary Hub\, The Comics Journal\, Maclean’
 s\, The Globe and Mail\, The Toronto Star\,\nand elsewhere.\n\nAndré Baby
 n is the author of\nEvie of the Deepthorn.\nHis work has appeared in\nPoet
 ry\, The Ex-Puritan\, Maisonneuve\n\, and elsewhere. He lives in Montréal
 \, where he teaches at McGill University.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:MAKE IT MAKE SENSE: THE FLYING BOOKS NONFICTION BOOKCLUB
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261124T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261124T203000
UID:bm-6629720261124@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Nov_nonfi
 c_book_club_2026.jpg?cb=1792687997
DESCRIPTION:[Book Club]\nJoin Tomas Hachard\, author of\nCity in Flames\n\
 , on Tuesday\, Novemeber 24th\, from 6:30 to 8:30 pm for a discussion of\n
 Women\, Walking\nedited by\, Zahra Ebrahim and Jane Farrow.\nWomen\, Walki
 ng\nexplores the broad spectrum of women’s experiences and perspectives 
 on walking\, from everyday strolls that let the mind breathe to ambitious 
 journeys that test courage and resolve.\nCo-edited by Zahra Ebrahim and Ja
 ne Farrow\, this anthology features thirty vignettes\, essays\, graphic ar
 t\, poems\, and interviews\, with contributors discussing identity\, love\
 , social connection\, grief\, and wonder. They each take us on a different
  path\, but share common ground in highlighting the deep significance to b
 e found in an ordinary walk.\nJoin us on Tuesday\, November 24th for an ev
 ening of conversation. We hope to see you there.\nGET YOUR TICKETS HERE!\n
 Your book club ticket includes:\nA glass of wine (or another beverage of y
 our choice).\nA thoughtful conversation with Tomas Hachard\, author of\nCi
 ty in Flames\n.\nA $20 credit toward any book in the shop.\nA few details:
 \nTicket price includes HST and fees.\n$20 book credit is valid on the nig
 ht of attendance only (non-refundable and non-transferable).\nWine is serv
 ed on the night of the event (ID required).\nTickets are non-refundable wi
 thin 7 days of the event\; due to limited capacity and planning\, we’re 
 not able to transfer tickets to future book club sessions.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF EBOLA\, BOMBS\, AND MIGRANTS BY DR JOANN
 E LIU
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261130T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261130T200000
UID:bm-6681920261130@flyingbooks.ca
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nDr. Joanne Liu presents the new release of\nEbo
 la\, Bombs\, and\nMigrants: Humanitarian Crises in a Security-Obsessed Wor
 ld\nin partnership with House of Anansi Press and Flying Books. Dr. Liu wi
 ll be joined in a fireside chat discussion with\nNew York Times\nbestselli
 ng\nauthor\, award-winning journalist\, and filmmaker Michelle Shephard\, 
 with an opportunity\nfor audience Q&A to follow.  The book will be avail
 able for sale with an opportunity to have\nthe book signed by Dr. Liu.\nJo
 in us for an incisive view with Dr. Joanne Liu\, former International\nPre
 sident of Médecins Sans Frontières (MSF) / Doctors Without Borders who w
 ill\ndiscuss her new book\nE\nbola\, Bombs\, and Migrants: Humanitarian Cr
 ises in a Security-\nObsessed World.\nDr. Liu details her frontline experi
 ence of three major humanitarian\ncrises during her tenure and reveals gro
 wing concerns in national security and its\ncapacity to overshadow our res
 ponsibility to protect vulnerable populations.\nJOANNE LIU has spent most 
 of the last thirty years working for Médecins Sans Frontières/Doctors Wi
 thout Borders\, where she also held term as international president. In 20
 15\, Liu was made an Officer of the Ordre national du Québec and was reco
 gnized by Time Magazine as one of the 100 Most Influential People of that 
 year. She is a recipient of several recognitions\, the Légion d'honneur o
 f the French Republic\, and the Pan American Health Organization (PAHO) Aw
 ard for Health Services Management and Leadership (2021)\, and the Meritor
 ious Service Cross of Canada.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
